course: dictionary
lesson: 450
title: Argue: two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word argue has two main meanings. The first is to speak angrily with someone because you disagree. «Brothers and sisters sometimes argue about whose turn it is on the computer.» The children are cross and do not agree. Shouting or hitting is never OK.
explain: The second meaning is to give reasons why you think something is true or right. «People argue whether the banana is a clever joke about money and art or proof that the art market is silly.» Nobody is shouting here. Each side gives reasons for its view.
explain: To work out which meaning you need, read the sentence around the word. Is someone cross, or are they giving reasons? Then swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Argue is a verb: argue, argues, argued, arguing.
fact: «Argue» means to speak angrily with someone because you disagree.
fact: «Argue» also means to give reasons why something is true or right.
fact: In a debate or a persuasive essay, you argue for or against something, using evidence.
fact: When you argue with a friend, take turns, listen and find a fair answer.
exercise: «Brothers and sisters sometimes argue about whose turn it is on the computer.» What does argue mean here? | To fall asleep | To give reasons why a film is good | *To speak angrily because you disagree | To sing loudly together
exercise: «People argue whether the banana is a clever joke about money and art or proof that the art market is silly.» What does argue mean here? | *To give reasons for different views | To shout and hit | To agree completely | To hide in a museum
exercise: In a debate, you must argue for or against the motion, using evidence. What does argue suggest? | Only saying how you feel | Running around the room | *Giving reasons with evidence | Going quiet and waiting
test: «Museums also raise big questions: some people argue about sending objects back to other countries.» In this sentence, argue means: | To copy the objects | To be cross and hide | *To give reasons for a view | To shout at the museum guide
test: Which is the best way to argue fairly with a friend? | Hit them if they disagree | Shout until they stop talking | Walk away and never speak again | *Listen, take turns and find a fair answer
exam: In «The referee's word is final: arguing can earn you a card», what does argue mean? | To win a prize | To give reasons why a rule is fair | To count the points | *To speak angrily to someone because you disagree
